         <work xml:id="work_d1e230227">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_fb6c60cd">B2:019</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_2d4010ed" xml:lang="de">In Festo Paschatos</title>
            <title type="alternative" xml:id="title_b856a8b9" xml:lang="de">Der Tod ist verschlungen in den Sieg</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_7f0cb900">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="_21">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_22_0">
               <p xml:id="idm105">The present Easter cantata is a transcript presumably made for or possibly by <persName xml:id="idm106" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Joseph Strohbach</persName> (1731–94) who studied and worked in Prague. During the 1760s he was a violinist in the city’s churches and amassed a large collection of sacred music. The dating of the manuscript, 27 April 1763, does not relate to Easter which fell at the beginning of the month and hence must refer to the copying rather than the event. It is possible that Scheibe was inspired by <persName xml:id="idm107" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName> who also composed an Easter cantata to a similar text. In that case, it would probably be an early work. Though it is possible that it is one of the Easter cantatas listed in the Breitkopf auction catalogue of 1836 (without providing a first line, however), the script does not agree with those found in the transcripts produced by Breitkopf.</p>
            </history>
